  "interpretation": {
    "finding": "On this LFW protocol, guard-enabled FaceNet exemplar matching retained 98.86% of known-class images and correctly identified all accepted known predictions; mean rejection of held-out unknown identities was 99.44%. Without the guard, mean known accuracy rose to 99.72% but every unknown image was accepted.",
    "comparison_with_same_features": "The calibrated SVM on identical FaceNet embeddings rejected all unknown test images, but its mean known-class coverage/accuracy was 94.87%, about 3.99 percentage points below guarded Neuron. Guarded Neuron's unknown rejection was 99.44%, 0.56 points below SVM; safe overall rate was 99.16% vs 97.47%.",
    "cnn_comparison_limit": "The from-scratch CNN trained for only 6 epochs on 354 known training images averaged 22.79% known-class accuracy. This is a weak/undertrained baseline and cannot be used as proof that Neuron beats CNNs. A fair architecture-level CNN comparison needs a stronger training recipe or an equivalent pretrained CNN backbone.",
    "external_validity_limit": "The benchmark covers only these selected LFW identities and may overlap identities with VGGFace2 pretraining. It tests the actual notebook guard policy with FaceNet embeddings, not every component of the broader Neuron architecture or general face recognition."
  }
